Another great example.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:42.440
The text says,
@RNGUB DANG DGANG DANG GZHIL BA DANG

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:49.700
He's quoting some elementary principals of pranayama.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:55.180
How to open your inner channels 
through breath practice.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.260
What it says here-- 
@RNGUB means inhale.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:04.460
@ DGANG means hold the breath, hold the prana.

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.980
And then @ GZHIL BA means expel it.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:14.040
And those are three steps 
of four steps of a certain pranayama practice.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:18.980
He only names three of the steps 
of a certain pranayama practice.

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.560
I'd like to say something about this practice.

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:32.060
Lots of people in yoga studios read a book about pranayama and they try to do some of it. 

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:40.660
And I've had yoga teachers who encouraged me to breathe in and then hold my breath as long as I can.

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:52.900
And then they would actually count 
and it was weird because I tried to do it 
and I thought they knew what they were talking about.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:56.640
I sincerely tried to do it.

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:59.780
Then I realized it was all a mistake.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.899
They read some book about pranayama.

00:14:02.899 --> 00:14:09.180
It doesn't do anything to help you,
 to try to hold your breath for as long as you can.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:17.480
If you want to do that then do laps under the pool, under the swimming pool and see if anything happens to your Buddha body or something.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:24.840
 It doesn't.  
It gave me headaches, it messed up my prana a little bit. It made me nervous.

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:30.240
These are mistaken practices.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:38.900
The goal of this breath practice here is called 
@kevala kumbhaka.

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:41.780
@kevala 
means spontaneous.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:50.560
@kumbhaka 
means a closed ceramic jar.

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:58.060
The goal is--
 when you're close to seeing emptiness directly,

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:04.560
your inner plumbing will fix itself 
and then when you see emptiness directly,

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:08.180
the prana does enter the central channel,

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.840
as if you were shooting an arrow.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:16.540
It enters the central channel.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:22.200
In the weeks before that or the days before that,

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:35.560
you might be, for example, at your university, 
and you're sitting in the library, and suddenly you realize you have not taken a breath for 10 or 15 minutes.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:38.860
And that's @kevala kumbaka.

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:45.180
What it says here @RNGUB DANG GZHIL BA.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.040
You don't have to do that.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:54.660
That's what happens. You do breathe in, you do hold it indefinitely, and then you do breathe it out.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:16:00.600
It's a very relaxed, spontaneous thing.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:10.020
One of the best ways to get your prana to shoot into your central channel like an arrow and stay there

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:14.780
is to be nice to other people,
and to be kind to other people.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:24.020
You don't have to-- 
it's not really the best method just to hold your breath and not think about other people, okay?

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:29.080
It would be better to be nice to everyone around you, 
to be kind to them.

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:37.220
And then that pranayama will take care of itself.
You can still do the different pranayamas.

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:45.680
What he's saying is, the core of that practice 
is love for other people and wanting 
to get other people out of trouble.

